% chr-hooks.elpi: what elpi-debug needs from Elpi's CHR engine and does
% not find there, as a small interface, written here as STAND-INS until
% Elpi has it (../ELPI-FEATURE-REQUESTS.md, §4-§6).  The display of CHR at work
% (chr.elpi) and the delay port (natives.elpi) go through this interface
% only; with the requested features, this file and the rewriting of the
% CHR rules by the driver (../bin/chr_in_view.ml) would go, or shrink to a
% few lines.
%
% THE INTERFACE
%
% - [start-rule-runner-if-needed]: called once at the start of [run]
%   (interpreter.elpi), under the local clauses of the run.  A goal of a
%   rule that fires is then run by [run-rule-goal Loc G] (chr.elpi), in
%   the interpreter, with the program of the run.  (§4: with a rule's goal
%   run in the current program, the driver would write the goal N of a
%   rule as [run-rule-goal Loc N] directly.)
% - [take-rule-notes Ns]: the attempts of rules since the last call, oldest
%   first: [tried Loc Kept Ks] (the rule at Loc tried on the constraints
%   numbered Ks, keeping the first Kept), [guard-held] (the attempt just
%   noted fires), [declared-at D] (a constraint declared at depth D, where
%   the attempts after it are shown).  [note-declaration Ctx]: a
%   constraint is about to be declared in the context Ctx (natives.elpi,
%   [delay]).  (§5: an event on each attempt and its outcome.)
% - [suspended-as K]: a local clause that [delay] installs around
%   declare_constraint, so that the suspended goal carries its number K.
%   [program-store Gs]: the goals of the store, each with its number, as
%   pairs [pr K G], most recent first; [store-readable]: the driver can
%   read the store.  (§6: the identity of a suspended goal, and the store
%   readable from Elpi.)
%
% THE STAND-INS (below), and what they cost
%
% - §4.  Elpi runs the goal of a rule with its static program only (elpi
%   4.0.0, runtime.ml, try_fire_rule: [initial_program]), where the local
%   clauses of [run] (the compiled rules, the options, the state), and so
%   the bridges, are missing.  The goal is handed to a runner: a goal
%   suspended at the start of the run, so with the program of [run],
%   which Elpi gives back to a woken goal.  The handing goes through CHR
%   itself, since a rule's goal reaches nothing of the run but the store.
%   Cost: the runner and its requests are constraints of the store
%   (hidden by [program-store], seen by print_constraints).
% - §5.  Elpi tells nothing of the rules it tries.  The driver rewrites
%   the guard G of each rule as [rule-tried Loc Kept Contexts, G,
%   rule-guard-held]: the notes of the attempt and of its outcome.  A
%   guard runs in a runtime of its own, with the static program only and
%   the variables of the constraints frozen: its notes go to a queue of
%   the driver (OCaml builtins), which the interpreter takes after the
%   propagation.  Not seen: the attempts whose heads do not match (Elpi
%   filters them before the guard); seen late: a stop at an attempt comes
%   after the propagation, the constraints removed already gone.
% - §6.  A suspended goal has no identity that Elpi code can read.  The
%   driver adds [suspended-as] to the context filter of each constraint
%   block, so that the context of a constraint holds the number of its
%   suspension: the guards read it there ([context-suspension]), and the
%   store, read by an OCaml builtin of the driver (API.RawData.constraints)
%   since Elpi has no builtin for it, gives it with each goal.  Cost: the
%   context of a constraint holds [suspended-as K] (seen by
%   print_constraints, and by a rule that names the context of a
%   constraint).


% ===== §6. The number of a suspension ===================================================

% [suspended-as K]: the goal suspended in this context has the number K
% (0 on the fast path, where suspensions are not numbered).  A local
% clause installed by [delay] around declare_constraint, so that it is in
% the context of the constraint: the guards of the rules and the store
% read it there.
pred suspended-as o:int.

% [context-suspension C K]: the number K of the suspension of the
% constraint whose context is C (the innermost [suspended-as]: Elpi lists
% the most recent clause first); 0 when it has none (a constraint declared
% by native code, or on the fast path).
pred context-suspension i:list (pred), o:int.
context-suspension C K :- std.mem C (suspended-as K1), !, K = K1.
context-suspension _ 0.

% ===== §4. The goals of the rules, handed to a runner =====================================
%
% The goal G of a rule becomes the constraint [rule-goal-request Loc G],
% which the rule below matches with the runner [rule-runner T]; its goal
% binds T, which wakes the runner, which runs G in the interpreter.  Both
% are suspended on _ (all such constraints share one key, and CHR only
% matches constraints that share a key), the runner also on T.

data rule-order.
symb run-goal reflect.srcloc -> (pred) -> rule-order.   % run the goal of the rule at Loc

% [hand-rule-goal Loc G]: the goal G of the rule at Loc, handed to the
% runner (the goal of each CHR rule of the program, as the driver writes it).
func hand-rule-goal reflect.srcloc, (pred).
hand-rule-goal Loc G :- declare_constraint (rule-goal-request Loc G) [_].

% [rule-goal-request Loc G]: the request to run G (a constraint only).
func rule-goal-request reflect.srcloc, (pred).

% [rule-runner T]: the runner, suspended on T; woken with T bound to
% [run-goal Loc G], it suspends itself again on a new variable (for the
% next request: one may come while G runs) and runs G, to its first
% solution (Elpi checks that the goal of a rule is a function: it has no
% other).
func rule-runner rule-order.
rule-runner (run-goal Loc G) :- start-rule-runner, run-rule-goal Loc G, !.

constraint rule-runner rule-goal-request {
  rule (rule-runner T) \ (rule-goal-request Loc G) <=> (T = run-goal Loc G).
}

% [start-rule-runner]: suspend the runner (at the start of [run], when the
% program has CHR rules).
func start-rule-runner.
start-rule-runner :- declare_constraint (rule-runner T) [T, _].

% [start-rule-runner-if-needed]: the runner, if the program has CHR rules.
pred start-rule-runner-if-needed.
start-rule-runner-if-needed :- reflect.chr-rule _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _, !, start-rule-runner.
start-rule-runner-if-needed.

% [own-constraint G]: G is a constraint of the runner, not of the program.
pred own-constraint i:any.
own-constraint (rule-runner _).
own-constraint (rule-goal-request _ _).

% ===== §5. The attempts, noted by the guards =============================================
%
% The steps added to a guard read the contexts of the constraints (which
% hold their numbers), bind nothing of the rule, declare no constraint,
% and always succeed: the rule behaves as before.

data rule-note.
symb tried reflect.srcloc -> int -> list int -> rule-note.
                    % the rule at Loc tried on the constraints numbered Ks,
                    % of which it keeps the first Kept (and removes the others if it fires)
symb guard-held rule-note.   % the guard of the attempt just noted held: the rule fires
symb declared-at int -> rule-note.
                    % a constraint is declared at this depth (the attempts after it
                    % are shown there, [delay], natives.elpi)

% [note-rule N], [take-rule-notes Ns]: keep a note, take the notes kept
% (oldest first).  No clause here: the driver elpi-debug (../bin) adds
% them, on its queue.
pred note-rule i:rule-note.
pred take-rule-notes o:list rule-note.

% [rule-tried Loc Kept Contexts]: the first step of the guard of the rule
% at Loc: the note of its attempt on the constraints whose contexts are
% Contexts.
pred rule-tried i:reflect.srcloc, i:int, i:list (list (pred)).
rule-tried Loc Kept Contexts :- std.map Contexts context-suspension Ks, note-rule (tried Loc Kept Ks), !.
rule-tried _ _ _.

% [rule-guard-held]: the last step of the guard: it held.
pred rule-guard-held.
rule-guard-held :- note-rule guard-held, !.
rule-guard-held.

% [note-declaration Ctx]: a constraint is about to be declared in the
% context Ctx (with events: its depth is where the attempts it leads to
% are shown).
:nooc
pred note-declaration o:ctx.
note-declaration _ :- quiet, !.
note-declaration (ctx D _) :- note-rule (declared-at D), !.
note-declaration _.

% ===== §6. The store, read by the driver ==================================================

% [constraint-store Gs]: the goals suspended in Elpi's store, each with
% the number of its suspension ([suspended-as] in its context, 0 if none),
% as pairs; a goal suspended under binders left since comes under as many
% λs.  No clause here: the driver elpi-debug adds it, on its builtin.
pred constraint-store o:list (pair int any).

% [store-readable]: the driver gives the store.
pred store-readable.
store-readable :- not (not (constraint-store _)).

% [program-store Gs]: the store, the runner left out.  Reading the store
% may bind (Elpi lowers the level of a variable of a goal suspended deeper
% than here): its callers undo that by failing ([show-store], [store-json]).
pred program-store o:list (pair int any).
program-store Gs :- constraint-store All, !, std.filter All program-constraint Gs.

% [program-constraint G]: the stored goal G is one of the program.
pred program-constraint i:pair int any.
program-constraint (pr _ G) :- not (own-constraint G).
